<h2>10. Spectroscopy Structure Solver Intelligence (52+ Benchmark Molecules)</h2>
<p>
  The Structure Solver is an algorithmic and database-driven deduction system that assists students in solving unknown molecular structures from multi-spectral data.
</p>

<h3>Curated MSc Chemistry Database (52+ Compounds)</h3>
<p>
  Contains complete spectral fingerprints ($^1	ext{H}$ NMR, $^{13}	ext{C}$ NMR, FT-IR, MS, UV-Vis) for over 52 postgraduate benchmark molecules:
  <em>Aspirin, Paracetamol, Benzoic Acid, Salicylic Acid, Benzaldehyde, Phenol, Aniline, Nitrobenzene, Toluene, Benzyl Alcohol, Acetophenone, Benzocaine, 1-Bromopropane, 2-Bromopropane, Vanillin, Ethyl propionate, Methyl salicylate, Ibuprofen, Pyridine, etc.</em>
</p>

<h3>4-Step Algorithmic Deduction Framework</h3>
<p>
  When an arbitrary molecular formula (e.g., $C_8H_8O$) is entered, the solver executes a 4-step deduction:
</p>
<div class="step-box">
  <p><span class="step-num">1</span> <strong>Degree of Unsaturation / Double Bond Equivalent (DBE / IHD):</strong></p>
  <div class="formula-box">$$	ext{DBE} = C + 1 - rac{H}{2} - rac{X}{2} + rac{N}{2}$$</div>
  <p>A DBE value of 4 or greater immediately indicates the presence of an aromatic ring ($C_6H_5$ or substituted core).</p>
</div>
<div class="step-box">
  <p><span class="step-num">2</span> <strong>FT-IR Functional Group Classification:</strong></p>
  <p>Identifies key diagnostic bands: carbonyls ($1680	ext{–}1750	ext{ cm}^{-1}$), hydroxyls ($3200	ext{–}3600	ext{ cm}^{-1}$), amines ($3300	ext{–}3500	ext{ cm}^{-1}$), and aromatic stretches ($1500	ext{–}1600	ext{ cm}^{-1}$).</p>
</div>
<div class="step-box">
  <p><span class="step-num">3</span> <strong>1H NMR Chemical Shift & Splitting Analysis:</strong></p>
  <p>Distinguishes constitutional isomers based on integration and coupling (e.g., Acetophenone's 3H singlet at $\delta 2.6$ vs Benzaldehyde's 1H singlet at $\delta 9.9$).</p>
</div>
<div class="step-box">
  <p><span class="step-num">4</span> <strong>Constitutional Isomer Elimination:</strong></p>
  <p>Explains systematically why alternative isomers matching the formula are ruled out based on the spectral evidence.</p>
</div>

